    {
      "id": "claim_3af3715d5daa45b2a4c0d4d3f5de3c31",
      "statement": "While it is possible to convert a Spring Boot uber jar into a Docker image with just a few lines in the Dockerfile, using the layering feature will result in an optimized image. When you create a jar containing the layers index file, the spring-boot-jarmode-tools jar will be added as a dependency to your jar. With this jar on the classpath, you can launch your application in a special mode which allows the bootstrap code to run something entirely different from your application, for example, something that extracts the layers.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://upstream/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/packaging/container-images/dockerfiles.adoc#L1-L101",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:52b81867d7e1548728f00958b7ce606c01c3be269394bddb725a85a1d1f1a7f3:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://upstream/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/partials/dockerfile",
          "version": "repo-file-v1:sha256:f786a8926628a0abf128af390728acac3a49a1db5422b96aacde57d160249503"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2445e9f23141434680e84bed78821ed5",
      "statement": "The extract command can be used to easily split the application into layers to be added to the Dockerfile. Here is an example of a Dockerfile using jarmode.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://upstream/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/packaging/container-images/dockerfiles.adoc#L1-L101",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:52b81867d7e1548728f00958b7ce606c01c3be269394bddb725a85a1d1f1a7f3:"
        }
